{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,320p' /app/agent-output/S18-terminal-sprite-render-trap/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# S18 — Terminal Sprite Render Trap\n\n## Findings\n\nThe symptoms are four different defects that happen to overlap visually:\n\n1. the ANSI background attribute is carried into a transparent half of a cell;\n2. a shorter row does not erase the longer row that preceded it;\n3. `tail2` contains hand-porting errors; and\n4. a self-rescheduling Node timer keeps some non-interactive CI processes alive.\n\n## 1. Phantom pixels\n\nThe renderer relies on terminal SGR state persisting from one cell to the next. In\nthe both-visible case it emits, in order:\n\n```text\nESC[38;2;<upper-r>;<upper-g>;<upper-b>m ESC[48;2;<lower-r>;<lower-g>;<lower-b>m ▀\n```\n\nThe `48;2;...m` sequence changes the terminal's current background color. It is\nnot scoped to that glyph and is not cleared by the next cell unless another escape\ndoes so.\n\nThe exposed cases are the half-filled cells:\n\n- `up !== undefined && lo === undefined` emits only `fg(up)` and `▀`. The lower,\n  empty half of `▀` is therefore painted with the previous cell's background.\n- `lo !== undefined && up === undefined` emits only `fg(lo)` and `▄`. The upper,\n  empty half of `▄` likewise uses the previous cell's background.\n\nAt the right edge of the outline, the sleep-Z marks, and the heart, those branches\ncan follow a cell that set a sprite-colored background. The result is a one-cell\ncolored patch in a half that is `.` in the frame data. `current` only suppresses\nduplicate escape strings; it does not make SGR state local to a cell. The row-end\n`RESET` is too late to repair an artifact already painted in the row.\n\nIf transparent means the terminal's default background, the exact minimal fix is\nto set that background explicitly in both one-sided branches:\n\n```ts\n} else if (up !== undefined) {\n  seq = fg(up) + '\\x1b[49m' // default background for the empty lower half\n  ch = '▀'\n} else if (lo !== undefined) {\n  seq = fg(lo) + '\\x1b[49m' // default background for the empty upper half\n  ch = '▄'\n```\n\nIf the sprite has a defined canvas background rather than the terminal default,\nuse `bg(canvasBackground)` instead of `\\x1b[49m`. The important contract is that\nevery half-block specifies the background used by its empty half; it must not\ninherit `48;2;...m` from a neighboring cell.\n\n## 2. Ghost frames\n\nFor each row the renderer emits a literal space for a fully transparent cell, then\ndoes:\n\n```ts\nout.replace(/[ ]+$/, '')\n```\n\nThat drops every trailing blank cell after the last visible glyph. The later\n`RESET` is an escape sequence and has zero terminal-column width; it does not\nrestore the dropped cells or clear their old contents. The text pipeline also\nnormalizes/suppresses trailing whitespace when it handles the row text, so padding\nthat is merely represented by spaces cannot be relied upon to reach the terminal.\n\nWhen the wide frame was drawn, its tail occupied real terminal cells. Rewriting\nthe row with a narrower string changes only the columns present in the new write.\nTerminal output is not a replacement-string operation: columns beyond the new\nend retain the old glyphs and colors. Hence the wide tail survives a switch even\nthough the narrow frame's own data and rendered prefix are correct.\n\nThe clean-switch fix has two parts:\n\n1. Preserve the canvas geometry end to end. Render every row as exactly 40\n   terminal cells, including its right-side blank cells, remove the renderer's\n   trailing-space trim, and use a text path that preserves those cells (or emits\n   an equivalent control sequence rather than whitespace padding).\n2. Erase the old terminal row/rectangle as part of the frame write. For a cursor\n   positioned at the row start, `\\x1b[2K\\r` followed by the row is a robust row\n   operation; `\\x1b[K` is sufficient when the cursor is already at the start and\n   the intended erase is to end of line. Then write the row at the known origin.\n\nFixed-width overwrite handles the known 40-column canvas, while explicit erase\nhandles a previously wider write and remains correct even if an intermediate\ntext layer strips whitespace. Both are part of the renderer contract; a final\n`RESET` only restores attributes and is not an erase operation.\n\n## 3. Frame data drift\n\nThe digest report says every listed frame matches source art except `tail2`:\n`standard`, `blink`, `fin1`, `fin2`, `spout1`–`spout6`, `tail1`, `tail3`,\n`tail4`, `heart1`–`heart3`, and `sleep1`–`sleep5` are `OK`. `tail2` has 23\ndifferent cells, all in the upper-right spout/tail-tip area. The report identifies\nthe pattern as a one-column-left shift: row 2 has the ported `D` at column 26\ninstead of source column 27; row 3 has an extra `D` at 25, a shifted `DBD` at\n26–28, and extra `DD` at 36–37; rows 4–6 continue the shifted upper-right\ncluster.\n\nThe existing regression was per-excerpt and was added for a different frame. It\ntherefore never compared the complete `tail2` 25-row frame with its source. A\npassing excerpt test could not detect an uncovered hand-copied region.\n\nThe gate should maintain a canonical digest manifest (or equivalent canonical\npixel fixtures) for every named frame and, in CI, hash/compare all 25 rows and\nthe expected 40-column geometry. A port or art change must fail the gate unless\nthe source art and the deliberately reviewed golden digest are changed together.\n`tail2` must first be corrected to source art; its bad digest must not be blessed\nas the new expected value.\n\n## 4. The hang\n\nThe animation planner schedules the next tick with `setTimeout`, and each tick\nschedules another timeout while its component remains mounted. That creates one\nlive Timeout handle at a time, but it never reaches a terminal state. The\nchannel-ui scene/check hosts mount the header component and finish their checks\nwithout unmounting it, so PASS is printed while the planner's timer chain still\npins the Node event loop. The runner eventually kills the otherwise idle process.\n\nThe interactive terminal does not reveal this as a new hang because its process\nalready has live TTY/stdin handles; those handles keep it alive regardless of the\nanimation timer chain. That masks the timer lifecycle bug in normal interactive\nuse; it does not make the timer safe for finite CI/test processes.\n\nFor this specific “finished but does not exit” failure, the one-line Node fix is\nto unref each scheduled timeout:\n\n```ts\nsetTimeout(tick, delay).unref()\n```\n\nThe timer may still reschedule while mounted, but it no longer prevents a process\nwith no other work from exiting. The complementary component contract should\nstill retain the timeout handle and `clearTimeout` it on unmount, so unref is not\nused as a substitute for lifecycle cleanup.\n\n## 5. Prevention\n\n### Renderer contract checklist\n\n- Define the canvas as 25 pixel rows × 40 pixel columns; every frame must satisfy\n  that geometry, even when its visible content is narrower.\n- Define `.` as transparent and define what transparent means on the terminal\n  (default background or an explicit canvas background).\n- For each half-block, set the foreground and background attributes required by\n  both halves. In particular, never leave an empty half dependent on a previous\n  cell's SGR background.\n- Treat SGR state as persistent terminal state: use explicit transitions, keep\n  the `current` optimization semantically correct, and reset/restore state at a\n  row or frame boundary.\n- Emit rows at fixed width through the text pipeline. No renderer or formatter\n  may silently trim the trailing blank cells.\n- On a frame rewrite, draw at a known cursor origin and erase the old row/region\n  or overwrite its full maximum extent. `RESET` is not erasure.\n- Keep all frames in canonical source-art form and gate ports with a digest or\n  pixel-for-pixel comparison covering every frame, row, and column.\n- Start animation only for a mounted/active component, cancel its timer on\n  unmount, and make background timers non-pinning in Node test/CI hosts (for\n  example with `unref`).\n- Test neighboring-cell SGR carry-over, upper-only and lower-only cells, wide to\n  narrow and narrow to wide transitions, every frame digest, terminal cursor /\n  erase behavior, and clean process exit.\n\n### Audit before enabling animation by default\n\n1. Inventory every consumer and host, including scene tests, snapshot runners,\n   CLI checks, and the interactive terminal. Record who mounts the component,\n   who unmounts it, and whether the host has a TTY.\n2. Run the all-frame digest/geometry gate against source art and review any\n   hand-ported frame changes. Include the currently mismatching `tail2` case in\n   the test corpus.\n3. Capture raw ANSI output in a terminal emulator for all frame transitions,\n   especially wide → narrow. Assert that no colored pixel appears outside the\n   frame mask and that old rows are erased.\n4. Verify the complete output path, not just the renderer: check that row\n   padding survives formatting/logging and that the frame writer uses explicit\n   erase semantics.\n5. Run finite CI hosts with real/fake timers and assert that they exit after\n   PASS. Check timer cleanup on unmount and the Node timer `unref` behavior;\n   compare runtime and open handles with the pre-animation baseline.\n6. Roll the default-on change through a guarded/staged run, with the digest,\n   ANSI artifact, lifecycle, and process-exit checks required before removing\n   the flag.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
